        "A straw man is when you weaken your opponent's argument to make it easier to defeat. A steelman is the opposite — you state their argument in its strongest possible form, even stronger than they stated it themselves, and then you defeat that. This is the mark of an honest apologist. 'The first that is in his own cause seemeth just; but his neighbour cometh and searcheth him' (Proverbs 18:17). If your defense of Adventism can only survive against weak versions of the opposition's arguments, then your defense is itself weak.\n\nThis week, you will learn to articulate the strongest possible case against every distinctive Adventist doctrine. You will argue like a former SDA, think like a critic, and construct the most devastating challenges you can imagine. Only after you have done this honestly will you be equipped to respond effectively. 'Prove all things; hold fast that which is good' (1 Thessalonians 5:21). If our doctrines are true, they will survive the strongest scrutiny. If they cannot survive honest examination, they do not deserve our allegiance.",
      avatarPresence:
        "I appreciate that you're trying to understand our arguments, but most Adventists build straw men out of our concerns and then pat themselves on the back for knocking them down. If you really want to engage, you need to understand that our best arguments aren't based on emotion — they're based on serious scholarship and careful biblical study.",
      tacticalBriefing:
        "Steelmanning is a discipline that makes you a stronger apologist. When you can articulate the opposition's argument better than they can, three things happen: (1) Your opponent feels respected and is more likely to listen to your response. (2) You discover genuine weaknesses in your own position that need strengthening. (3) Your eventual response addresses the real objection, not a caricature. Today's focus is on the methodology of steelmanning: how to research the strongest critics, how to identify the core of an argument beneath its emotional packaging, and how to state the argument fairly without conceding your own position.",
      drill:
        "Choose any one Adventist distinctive doctrine (Sabbath, state of the dead, Investigative Judgment, Ellen White's prophetic gift, or health message). Research the strongest scholarly critique of that doctrine from former SDA sources. Write a 300-word steelmanned version of the critique — one so strong that an Adventist reading it might feel uncomfortable. Then write one sentence explaining why steelmanning this argument makes you a better defender of it.",
      forgeAWeapon:
        "Create a 'Steelman Template' — a structured format for honestly presenting any opponent's argument. Include sections for: (a) The Core Claim, (b) The Strongest Evidence, (c) The Emotional Appeal, (d) Why This Argument Is Persuasive, and (e) The Concession (what kernel of truth exists in the argument).",
      jeevesDebrief:
        "You have begun the most intellectually honest exercise in all of apologetics. Many defenders of faith are afraid to state the opposition's case strongly because they fear it will shake their own convictions. But the truth is the opposite: doctrines that survive the strongest critique emerge stronger, not weaker. As iron sharpens iron, so the steelmanned objection sharpens the apologist's response. 'Iron sharpeneth iron; so a man sharpeneth the countenance of his friend' (Proverbs 27:17).",
