      instructorVoice:
        "This is perhaps the most pervasive logical error in the former SDA's arsenal, and it is almost always unconscious. The reasoning runs like this: 'I had a terrible experience in the Adventist church. Therefore, Adventist doctrine is wrong.' The conclusion does not follow from the premise — but it feels like it does, because the emotional pain associated with the experience colors everything connected to it. This is a textbook case of the association fallacy, and it is devastatingly effective because it operates below the level of conscious reasoning.\n\nConsider: if a medical student has a terrible experience at a teaching hospital — bullying professors, exhausting hours, institutional politics — does that mean the medical knowledge taught there is false? Obviously not. The quality of the instruction is independent of the quality of the institutional culture. Yet former SDAs routinely reject the Sabbath because they were shamed for playing on Sabbath, reject Ellen White because an overbearing teacher weaponized her quotes, and reject the health message because church members were judgmental about diet.\n\n'Cease, my son, to hear the instruction that causeth to err from the words of knowledge' (Proverbs 19:27). The 'instruction that causeth to err' is not the doctrine itself — it is the false conclusion drawn from painful association.",
