        "When all other arguments are exhausted, the former SDA will often deploy what may be their most impregnable defense: 'I'm happier now.' This statement functions as both a testimony and a trump card. It is experiential, unfalsifiable, and deeply personal. How do you argue with someone's happiness? The answer is: you do not argue with it. You acknowledge it — and then you gently explore whether happiness is the ultimate criterion for truth.\n\nConsider Jonah. He was happier sitting under his gourd than he was preaching to Nineveh. God let the gourd grow — and then He sent a worm to destroy it. The gourd of comfort was not God's will for Jonah; obedience to the prophetic commission was. 'Then said the LORD, Thou hast had pity on the gourd, for the which thou hast not laboured, neither madest it grow; which came up in a night, and perished in a night' (Jonah 4:10). Happiness built on avoidance of truth is a gourd — green today, withered tomorrow.\n\n'There is a way which seemeth right unto a man, but the end thereof are the ways of death' (Proverbs 14:12). Not every path that feels right is right. Not every sense of peace is genuine peace. The Christian's compass is Scripture, not subjective emotional states.",
